From bbd5b230301a70c46afa2b5e4b682b3515678549 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dev <dev@local>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 22:32:47 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(map): react-konva board with multi-map switching and
 synced tokens

Implements the 2D map engine the architecture reserved a slot for.

Maps are campaign documents, modelled on handouts: one Markdown file per
map under maps/ whose frontmatter names an image in assets/. The GM reveals
one at a time; activation is exclusive and written to disk, so a client that
reconnects reads which map is live rather than being told by another client.

Tokens are session state, never persisted. A drag emits a position every
animation frame, and rewriting Markdown at that rate would be slow and
meaningless between sessions. They live in the host's memory, are dropped
when their owner disconnects, and are authorised per operation: a player may
move only their own piece, the GM may move any.

Holding 60 FPS drove three decisions. Positions bypass React entirely and are
written straight to the Konva node, so a moving board causes no re-renders.
The board is split across three layers repainted on separate schedules: the
map on reveal, the tokens on drag, the save markers on the pulse. Movement is
a fire-and-forget broadcast rather than an RPC, coalesced to one message per
frame.

A player places themselves by dragging their profile picture onto the board;
that picture is now a real portrait, from an optional portrait: field on the
sheet, falling back to initials on the profile colour. The feature is off for
the GM, who runs the table rather than standing on it.

Also:
- crossfade moved inside the canvas, so tokens no longer fade with the map
- handout windows open larger and are resizable from the corner
- the handout drawer opens on the toolbar side, clear of the chat panel
- bundle handouts, maps and assets as Tauri resources; only sheets shipped
  before, so handouts worked in dev and vanished from packaged builds
